```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
    // every Shape shares this real invariant: area() >= 0
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;
    double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}
class Square extends Shape {
    double side;
    double area() { return side * side; }
}
```

This hierarchy is closed (a fixed, known set of shapes), and every subtype honors the same real invariant — `area()` is always non-negative, and no subclass needs to override that promise away. There's no combinatorial second axis, and no plausible future subtype breaks the contract.

```java
interface AreaCalculator { double area(); }
class CircleAreaCalculator implements AreaCalculator {
    double radius;
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}
class Shape {
    private final AreaCalculator calculator;
    Shape(AreaCalculator calculator) { this.calculator = calculator; }
    double area() { return calculator.area(); }
}
```

This compiles and works, but it's strictly worse here: there's now an extra indirection layer and an extra object to construct for every shape, and none of it buys anything — there was no variant threatening to break the contract, and no second independent axis to combine. Composition's cost with none of its benefit.
